-- One story each, both real, dated and independently sourced.
--
-- Uzbekistan: the AI SOLIQ launch of 29 July 2026 -- twelve days old
-- at build time, and the natural follow-on to the January 2026
-- risk-scoring launch. Deliberately chosen over the risk-scoring
-- launch itself, which is already a milestone on the timeline; this
-- project's convention is that stories carry news, not restate
-- milestones.
--
-- Azerbaijan: the 1 January 2026 invoice-timing change. This is a
-- live compliance break that the major international e-invoicing
-- trackers have not covered, and one widely-cited industry summary
-- still describes the superseded blanket five-day rule -- which makes
-- it exactly the kind of thing this site exists to catch. Sourced to
-- the State Tax Service's own 2026 amendments FAQ.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, date, month, summary_en, html_en, source_url, published) VALUES ('2026-07-29-uzbekistan-ai-soliq', '2026-07-29', '2026-07', 'Uzbekistan''s Tax Committee has launched AI SOLIQ, an AI platform built on its own "Soliq LLM" model. Its most consequential feature for businesses: taxpayers can now ask the system why a specific electronic invoice was flagged as high-risk under the real-time scoring regime that went live in January 2026 — criteria that are otherwise deliberately unpublished.', '<h3>🇺🇿 Uzbekistan builds a chatbot to explain the invoice risk scores it will not publish</h3>
<p>On 29 July 2026 Uzbekistan''s Tax Committee unveiled AI SOLIQ, a unified artificial-intelligence platform built on "Soliq LLM", its own proprietary language model, with text and speech generation, computer vision and machine learning components.</p>
<p>The feature that matters most to anyone trading in Uzbekistan is narrower than the launch billing suggests. Since 1 January 2026, every electronic invoice issued in the country has been scored for risk in real time and flagged red or green, under Article 4 of Presidential Decree UP-153. The criteria — reportedly 48 of them — are deliberately withheld, on international experts'' advice, to stop taxpayers gaming the system. That secrecy is defensible as anti-avoidance policy and awkward as customer service: a business could see an invoice flagged with no way to find out why.</p>
<p>AI SOLIQ is the answer to that. Taxpayers can now hold a dialogue with the system about why a specific invoice was classified red, alongside explanations of their tax rating and filing obligations. The stakes are real rather than reputational: input VAT on a flagged invoice becomes creditable only once the tax has actually reached the budget, so a supplier''s risk status directly delays its customers'' cash flow.</p>
<p>It is a striking sequence — a tax administration that first made invoice risk consequential and unexplained, then built a language model to explain it — and one worth watching as other clearance and CTC jurisdictions add scoring layers of their own.</p>', 'https://www.uzdaily.uz/ru/nalogovyi-komitet-uzbekistana-predstavil-ai-soliq/', 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO story_countries (story_id, country_id) SELECT '2026-07-29-uzbekistan-ai-soliq', id FROM countries WHERE code = 'UZ';
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO story_translations (story_id, lang, title, summary, html) VALUES ('2026-07-29-uzbekistan-ai-soliq', 'en', 'Uzbekistan builds a chatbot to explain the invoice risk scores it will not publish', 'Uzbekistan''s Tax Committee has launched AI SOLIQ, an AI platform built on its own "Soliq LLM" model. Its most consequential feature for businesses: taxpayers can now ask the system why a specific electronic invoice was flagged as high-risk under the real-time scoring regime that went live in January 2026 — criteria that are otherwise deliberately unpublished.', '<h3>🇺🇿 Uzbekistan builds a chatbot to explain the invoice risk scores it will not publish</h3>
<p>On 29 July 2026 Uzbekistan''s Tax Committee unveiled AI SOLIQ, a unified artificial-intelligence platform built on "Soliq LLM", its own proprietary language model, with text and speech generation, computer vision and machine learning components.</p>
<p>The feature that matters most to anyone trading in Uzbekistan is narrower than the launch billing suggests. Since 1 January 2026, every electronic invoice issued in the country has been scored for risk in real time and flagged red or green, under Article 4 of Presidential Decree UP-153. The criteria — reportedly 48 of them — are deliberately withheld, on international experts'' advice, to stop taxpayers gaming the system. That secrecy is defensible as anti-avoidance policy and awkward as customer service: a business could see an invoice flagged with no way to find out why.</p>
<p>AI SOLIQ is the answer to that. Taxpayers can now hold a dialogue with the system about why a specific invoice was classified red, alongside explanations of their tax rating and filing obligations. The stakes are real rather than reputational: input VAT on a flagged invoice becomes creditable only once the tax has actually reached the budget, so a supplier''s risk status directly delays its customers'' cash flow.</p>
<p>It is a striking sequence — a tax administration that first made invoice risk consequential and unexplained, then built a language model to explain it — and one worth watching as other clearance and CTC jurisdictions add scoring layers of their own.</p>');

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, date, month, summary_en, html_en, source_url, published) VALUES ('2026-01-01-azerbaijan-invoice-timing', '2026-01-01', '2026-01', 'Azerbaijan has closed its five-day service invoicing window for two categories. From 1 January 2026, international transport must be invoiced before the transport begins, and continuous services must be invoiced monthly regardless of the contractual billing cycle. Several international compliance summaries still describe the superseded blanket five-day rule.', '<h3>🇦🇿 Azerbaijan closes the five-day window for continuous services and international transport</h3>
<p>A law of 9 December 2025, applied by Presidential Decree No. 563 of 29 December 2025, inserted two new articles into Azerbaijan''s Tax Code with effect from 1 January 2026. Together they end the blanket five-day deadline that has governed service invoicing under the e-qaimə regime.</p>
<p>International transport services must now be invoiced by the time the transport begins — reversing the usual order of operations for freight and logistics operators, who are used to invoicing after the fact. Regularly and continuously supplied services must be invoiced for each calendar month at the point that month''s service provision begins, even where the contract provides for quarterly or annual billing. The State Tax Service''s own guidance uses a security-services contract as its worked example; the same logic catches cleaning, utilities, subscriptions and most software-as-a-service arrangements.</p>
<p>The stated rationale is straightforward anti-avoidance: taxpayers had been using the five-day grace period to slide transactions across reporting-period boundaries.</p>
<p>What makes this worth flagging is how quietly it landed. Azerbaijan''s mandate has been universal since 2018 and is well established, so it attracts little international coverage — and at least one widely-cited compliance summary still states a blanket five-day rule as though nothing changed. Any business billing an Azerbaijani customer on a quarterly or annual cycle for a continuous service now has an invoicing cadence that is out of step with the law, whatever its contract says.</p>', 'https://www.taxes.gov.az/uploads/2026/faq/FAQ2026.pdf', 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO story_countries (story_id, country_id) SELECT '2026-01-01-azerbaijan-invoice-timing', id FROM countries WHERE code = 'AZ';
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO story_translations (story_id, lang, title, summary, html) VALUES ('2026-01-01-azerbaijan-invoice-timing', 'en', 'Azerbaijan closes the five-day window for continuous services and international transport', 'Azerbaijan has closed its five-day service invoicing window for two categories. From 1 January 2026, international transport must be invoiced before the transport begins, and continuous services must be invoiced monthly regardless of the contractual billing cycle. Several international compliance summaries still describe the superseded blanket five-day rule.', '<h3>🇦🇿 Azerbaijan closes the five-day window for continuous services and international transport</h3>
<p>A law of 9 December 2025, applied by Presidential Decree No. 563 of 29 December 2025, inserted two new articles into Azerbaijan''s Tax Code with effect from 1 January 2026. Together they end the blanket five-day deadline that has governed service invoicing under the e-qaimə regime.</p>
<p>International transport services must now be invoiced by the time the transport begins — reversing the usual order of operations for freight and logistics operators, who are used to invoicing after the fact. Regularly and continuously supplied services must be invoiced for each calendar month at the point that month''s service provision begins, even where the contract provides for quarterly or annual billing. The State Tax Service''s own guidance uses a security-services contract as its worked example; the same logic catches cleaning, utilities, subscriptions and most software-as-a-service arrangements.</p>
<p>The stated rationale is straightforward anti-avoidance: taxpayers had been using the five-day grace period to slide transactions across reporting-period boundaries.</p>
<p>What makes this worth flagging is how quietly it landed. Azerbaijan''s mandate has been universal since 2018 and is well established, so it attracts little international coverage — and at least one widely-cited compliance summary still states a blanket five-day rule as though nothing changed. Any business billing an Azerbaijani customer on a quarterly or annual cycle for a continuous service now has an invoicing cadence that is out of step with the law, whatever its contract says.</p>');
